## Round 0 — p1, first launch stopped and relaunched

[18] 20:20 `fw spawn-worker p1` launched. My Bash tool moved it to a background task with
     a 30-minute cap (the tool's default), which is far short of a worker round: by minute
     14 the worker had written solution.py, tests and an experiment driver and was only
     just starting its first ZINC run.
[19] Rather than let it be killed mid-round at minute 30, I stopped it at minute 15 and
     relaunched with an explicit 2-hour background budget, which is the longest a single
     Bash call can hold. Verified afterwards that no fw/claude process from the stopped
     run survived (only my own master process remains), so there is no second worker
     writing into problems/p1.
[20] fw restarts a respawned problem at round 1 with a fresh agent session, so the new
     worker will find the stopped attempt's files in its playground with no memory of
     writing them. I appended section 6 to problems/p1/problem.md saying exactly that:
     the files are reusable but unvalidated, the interrupted run produced no trustworthy
     numbers, and it should re-run every experiment itself.
[21] Cost of the restart: ~15 minutes of wall clock. Worth it against a near-certain kill
     at minute 30, which would have landed in the middle of the first training run.

